sec.p:
From the simulation trajectories comprising the coordinates of at least 7 × 104 polymer conformations sampled over time, R(s), MSD1(t) and MSD2(t) were calculated for s = 58, 82, 88, 149, 195, 595, 3327 kb using Eq. 1, Eq. 2, and Eq. 3 respectively.
(1)
R(s)=⟨‖R‖⟩ \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} R(s)=\langle\|\mathbf{R}\|\rangle \tag{(1} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where R is the distance vector between the two loci.
(2)
MSD1(t)=⟨‖X(t0+t)−X(t0)‖2⟩t0 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \operatorname{MSD}_{1}(t)=\left\langle\left\|\mathbf{X}\left(t_{0}+t\right)-\mathbf{X}\left(t_{0}\right)\right\|^{2}\right\rangle_{t_{0}} \tag{2} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where X is the position vector of a locus, t0 is the initial time, and t is the lag time.
(3)
MSD2(t)=⟨‖R(t0+t)−R(t0)‖2⟩t0 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \operatorname{MSD}_{2}(t)=\left\langle\left\|\mathbf{R}\left(t_{0}+t\right)-\mathbf{R}\left(t_{0}\right)\right\|^{2}\right\rangle_{t_{0}} \tag{3} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
The effect of the localization error of 180 nm in [42, 70] was tested on R(s) computed from various simulation trajectories to ensure that this factor could not affect the fits significantly. Indeed, the effects of localization error were negligible (Fig. S5).

The relaxation time τ (s) was measured from the simulations by computing the time taken for the autocorrelation of the R for each s (4) to decay to a specific value 5. The scaling exponent was then estimated by taking the slope of τ (s) plotted on a logarithmic scale.
(4)
C(t)=⟨R(t0)⋅R(t0+t)⟩t0⟨‖R(t0)‖2⟩t0 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} C(t)=\frac{\left\langle\mathbf{R}\left(t_{0}\right) \cdot \mathbf{R}\left(t_{0}+t\right)\right\rangle_{t_{0}}}{\left\langle\left\|\mathbf{R}\left(t_{0}\right)\right\|^{2}\right\rangle_{t_{0}}} \tag{4} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(5)
τ=t|C(t)=C(t=0)e \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \tau=t \left\lvert\, C(t)=\frac{C(t=0)}{e}\right.$$\end{document}
The Hi-C map for each simulation was generated using the contactmaps.binnedContactMap function of the polychrom package [71], which bins monomer-resolution contacts to 16 kb resolution based on the input contact threshold. The contact map was then normalized using Sequential Component Normalization [72] and used to compute Pc(s).

The comparison of MFETs from the block copolymer simulations with the reference Rouse model with excluded volume (MFETref) was done by computing the measure given in 7.
(7)
MFETdiv=log⁡2MFETrefMFETd \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathrm{MFET}_{\text {div }}=\log 2 \frac{\mathrm{MFET}_{\text {ref }}}{\mathrm{MFET}_{\text {d }}} \tag{7} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

This procedure was also used for MSD1(t), with the sole difference being that the initial polynomial fitting used a first-degree instead of a fifth-degree polynomial.
(10)
σMSD2=∑s=58kb3.3Mb|MSD2exp(t)+ΔMSD2−Fs(t+Δt)| \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \sigma_{\mathrm{MSD}_{2}}=\sum_{s=58 \mathrm{~kb}}^{3.3 \mathrm{Mb}}\left|\operatorname{MSD}_{2}^{\text {exp }}(t)+\Delta_{\mathrm{MSD}_{2}}-\mathcal{F}_{s}\left(t+\Delta_{t}\right)\right| \tag{10} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
As with spatial units, real time units (in seconds, s) can be converted to simulation time units (timestep of the Langevin integrator, t.u.) and vice versa using the temporal scaling factor Δt obtained from the fitting procedure (Eq. 11). For the best-fit case of loop extrusion, we obtained 10−Δt=1278.71\begin{document}$10^{-\Delta_{t}}=1278.71$\end{document}, implying that 1 s = 1278.71 t.u.
(11)
texpt.u.=texps×10−Δtt.u.s−1 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} t_{\text {exp }} \text { t.u. }=t_{\text {exp }} \mathrm{s} \times 10^{-\Delta_{t}} \text { t.u. } \mathrm{s}^{-1} \tag{11} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
To estimate the deviation σP\begin{document}$\sigma_{\mathrm{P}}$\end{document} between the simulated and the experimental Hi-C data, we computed the sum of absolute differences between the two curves for equally spaced s values on a logarithmic scale. The correlation (both Spearman’s and Pearson) was calculated between the two corresponding observed-over-expected maps as described by Eq. 12.
(12)
ρ[OE(SCN(Msim)),OE(SCN(Mexp))] \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \rho\left[\operatorname{OE}\left(\operatorname{SCN}\left(M_{\text {sim }}\right)\right), \operatorname{OE}\left(\operatorname{SCN}\left(M_{\exp }\right)\right)\right] \tag{12} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where Mx denotes a Hi-C map, SCN is the normalization function [72], and OE is the observed-over-expected function. The latter involves calculating, for each value on the Hi-C map, the log ratio of the expected value estimated based on its corresponding s.

The stochastic model was implemented such that the ratio between the probabilities of LEF loading pL and unloading pU determined the steady-state mean occupancy of LEFs ⟨Ω⟩ on the lattice (Eq. 14). The steady state was dependent on the size of the lattice and the sliding probability of the LEFs pS, and was achieved for time t >> 104 t.u. Therefore, to vary ⟨Ω⟩, pL was varied while keeping the sliding probability pS and pU constant. pS = 0.5 and pU = 0.01 were assigned to attain a mean LEF processivity λ=2×pSpU≈100kb\begin{document}$\lambda=2 \times \frac{p_{S}}{p_{U}} \approx 100 \mathrm{~kb}$\end{document}.
dΩdt=pL−pUΩ \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \frac{d \Omega}{d t}=p_{L}-p_{U} \Omega$$\end{document}
Steady state achieved when dΩdt=0\begin{document}$\frac{d \Omega}{d t}=0$\end{document}, therefore
(14)
⟨Ω⟩=pLpU \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \langle\Omega\rangle=\frac{p_{L}}{p_{U}} \tag{14} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
From Eq. 11 and the average time taken for a LEF to slide 1 kb (800 t.u., Table 1), LEF parameters were estimated in real units. Depending on the simulation case, 1105≤10−Δt≤1661\begin{document}$1105 \leq 10^{-\Delta_{t}} \leq 1661$\end{document} t.u. s−1. Taking the best-fit case for dynamics (⟨Ω⟩ = 0.95 LEFs per 100 kb), we find
(15)
Δt=−3.10710−Δt=1279t.u.s−1 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} \Delta_{t} & =-3.107 \\ 10^{-\Delta_{t}} & =1279 \text { t.u. } \mathrm{s}^{-1} \tag{(15} \end{align*}$$\end{document}
Using this conversion factor, we estimated the values for mean LEF velocity as vLEF ≈ kb s−1 (Eq. 16) and mean LEF residence time as τLEF ≈ 63 s (Eq. 17).
(16)
vLEF=pS×10−Δtt.u.s−1800t.u.=0.5×1279t.u.s−1800t.u.≈0.8kbs−1 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} v_{\mathrm{LEF}}=p_{S} \times \frac{10^{-\Delta_{t}} \text { t.u. } \mathrm{s}^{-1}}{800 \text { t.u. }}=0.5 \times \frac{1279 \text { t.u. } \mathrm{s}^{-1}}{800 \text { t.u. }} \approx 0.8 \mathrm{~kb} \mathrm{~s}^{-1} \tag{16} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(17) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \tau_{\mathrm{LEF}}=\frac{1}{p_{U}} \times 800 \text { t.u. } \times 10^{\Delta_{t}} \mathrm{~s} \mathrm{t.u.}{ }^{-1}=\frac{1}{0.01} \times 800 \text { t.u. } \times 10^{-3.107} \mathrm{~s} \mathrm{t.u.}{ }^{-1} \approx 63 \mathrm{~s} \tag{17} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
